From e1ebb6dc94215bff6910f073f39b06f9c3b0b6d5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:06:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] ci(mini): stop running hermes_state (macOS) on main
 pushes; schedule tests main

After #1538 path-filtered the standalone main-push trigger, required PR
`tests/hermes_state (macOS, mini)` jobs still waited p90 18 min (3/17 > 15
min). Every one of those waits queued behind a ~10 min main-push run on the
single mini runner.

Drop main from the push trigger. CI already runs the lane on the merge of
each PR into main, so the main push re-tested a tree PR CI had just covered.
The 4-hourly schedule still tests main HEAD and catches skew between PRs that
land close together, or a change outside the surface. ci-probe/** keeps an
unfiltered push trigger for probing the runner. ci.yaml keeps masking its
own main-push call, so main pushes don't come back that way.

Evidence (t_01a1d37abf5db0ae):
- 16 days of main-push runs: 437 completed. Of the 5 failures, none was a
  confirmed code regression (2 low disk, 1 runner loss, 1 host file timeout,
  1 never started).
- FIFO replay of the 9.5 h after #1538: PR-job p90 drops from 17.6 to 5.8 min
  (0/17 > 15). Cancel-in-progress on the push group changes nothing in that
  window, because main pushes never overlapped. Dropping Native memory alone
  only reaches p90 14.0, since its jobs are ~2 min.

The tests now assert that main pushes never start the lane, that ci-probe
still does, that CI's main-push mask is kept, and that the schedule backstop
works. The path-list/classifier equality tests are gone with the list.
